src('SRC069','341st Strategic Missile Wing histories, excerpts for 1 Jan-31 Mar 1967, 1 Apr-30 Jun 1967, 1 Jul-30 Sep 1967, 1 Oct-31 Dec 1967, 1 Jan-31 Mar 1968 (88-page FOIA release, J. Greenewald, 2001)','USAF 341st SMW historians (SAC history program); released by USAF under FOIA','The Black Vault (hosting the scanned release)','1967-1968 (released 2001)','Official unit histories, excerpts (scanned PDF, OCR text)','PRIMARY','http://documents.theblackvault.com/documents/ufos/malmstromufo.pdf','HQ SAC history series (DXIH)','Echo Flight investigation sections in all five volumes; I read the Q1, Q2 and parts of Q3/Q4 excerpts','%s'%A,'INC019;INC020;EVD131;EVD132;EVD133','Original scans (88 pp, OCR text noisy); Echo Flight investigation: 0845 shutdown, channels 9 and 12 No-Go, "No other Wing I configuration lost strategic alert at that time", UFO rumors disproven, Boeing/Autonetics pulse tests, power and EMP tests.','Excerpts only (about 84 pages of five volumes); OCR errors; I read perhaps a quarter of the text; absence of Oscar or other incidents in excerpts is not conclusive.','VERIFIED','EXAMINED')
ev('EVD131',"The Q2 1967 history (SRC069, original scan; OCR) confirms the Boeing/Autonetics reproduction: 'During testing at Boeing, a 30 micro sec Pulse (-10 to 0 volt square wave) was placed on the Self Test Command (STC) line at the C-53P Coupler Logic Driver interface ... Seven out of 10 separate applications of a single Pulse would cause the system to shut down with a Channel 9 & 12 No-Go.' Autonetics explained the mechanism (the pulse starts the coupler self-test but is too short to put the computer in coupler-test mode, giving a sequence error and G&C/coupler No-Go). EMP testing at the LF and Wing showed the Sensitive Information Network (SIN) lines susceptible to noise of the type that could have caused the problem; the SIN lines run only from the LCC to all the LFs in the flight, which 'could explain the flight peculiar aspect'; tests proved significant coupling between the SIN shields and the STC line. This verifies the 1996 Klotz/Salas statement that a 10-volt pulse on a data line reproduced the shutdown (EVD088), now from the primary document, and confirms that the cause was investigated as an electrical noise pulse, not a UFO.",'GOVERNMENT_RECORD','SRC069','INC019;GOV006;EVD088;CLM252','10099','CRITICAL','VERIFIED')
ev('EVD132',"The wing history's leading hypothesis and its negative result (SRC069, Q2 and Q3 1967; OCR): the 'best possible source of the noise Pulse' was a transformer failure in the Echo flight area; shorting of a single-phase transformer may have unbalanced the three-phase system enough to send ground currents back to the generator, the hardened intersite cable shields giving a path for voltage pulses on the SIN lines. Transformer-failure simulation tests were run at Malmstrom in the week of 15 May 1967 (two sites shut down for the test): 'no significant noise propagation' on the SIN lines or commercial power lines from simulating the transformer failure; a one-volt 60 Hz signal and a current spike were seen but judged insignificant because the Logic Coupler responds to pulses of 10-100 microseconds, not a 16.7 ms 60 Hz signal. The history concluded: 'Due to the fact that the power tests were essentially negative, it appears that the cause of the Echo Flight problem was of the EMP or electrostatic nature'; the Q3 history repeats that one of the primary theories was an adverse power effect and 'all tests ... proved negative' (EMP tests at Sierra-39 were the final series), with no cause found. So (a) the 'cascading transformer failure' later attributed to AARO officials is the Air Force's own 1967 hypothesis, tested and found essentially negative by the Air Force; (b) the contemporaneous history itself floats an EMP/electrostatic cause. I did not read the timing of the transformer event in this passage (the Q2 transcription gives a transformer short at ~1450, after the 0845 shutdown).",'GOVERNMENT_RECORD','SRC069','INC019;EVD085;EVD089;EVD090;CLM253','10099','CRITICAL','PARTIALLY_VERIFIED')
ev('EVD133',"Further checks of the wing history scans against the book's accounts: (a) The original scan confirms the rr0 transcription I used earlier (SRC002): 16 Mar 1967 0845; all Echo LFs lost alert 'nearly simultaneously'; 'No other Wing I configuration lost strategic alert at that time'; the crew's 10-40 s estimate; 'Rumors of Unidentified Flying Objects ... disproven' on the basis of the Mobile Strike Team that checked 'November Flight's' LFs (OCR). (b) The excerpts mention only the Echo event and a similar Alpha Flight event of 19 Dec 1966 (same capsule crew, adverse weather); I found no mention of an Oscar, Mike, November or India shutdown, but the volumes are excerpts, so this is not proof of absence. (c) The Q3 excerpt states 'There were no missile accidents or incidents during this quarter, but testing on the Echo Flight Incident was accomplished', so no multi-missile incident is recorded for that quarter (which quarter the OCR belongs to I did not confirm). (d) The Q4 1967 excerpt records a separate October 1967 LCF power/battery incident whose cause 'could not be discovered' and was 'believed to be a freak incident'. (e) Salas's claim that no Boeing final report was written (CLM036) is not tested here, but the history cites an Engineering Investigation Team report of 23 Mar 1967 and later Boeing/Autonetics and OOAMA reports and interim reports (e.g., 2 Jun 1967), so engineering reporting existed.",'GOVERNMENT_RECORD','SRC069','INC019;INC020;EVD085;GOV006;CLM036;RQ036','10099','HIGH','PARTIALLY_VERIFIED')
clm('CLM252',"Boeing/Autonetics reproduced the Echo shutdown (channels 9 and 12 No-Go) by applying a 30 microsecond, -10 to 0 V pulse to the self-test command line at the coupler, in 7 of 10 trials.","Wing history Q2 1967",'DOCUMENT','HIGH',10099,'VERIFIED')
clm('CLM253',"The Air Force's own 1967 leading hypothesis was a transformer failure inducing ground-current noise on the intersite cable shields; tests in May 1967 found no significant noise propagation, and the history concluded the cause was of EMP or electrostatic nature.","Wing history Q2/Q3 1967",'DOCUMENT','HIGH',10099,'VERIFIED')
